  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Electrons are transferred from a metal to a non-metal, and the resulting ions attract one another.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two atoms each contribute one electron to a shared pair.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The attraction between the ions and the mobile electrons holds the metal together.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ions must be free to move; in the solid lattice they are fixed.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Every outer electron of carbon is localised in a covalent bond.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each carbon is bonded to three others, leaving one delocalised electron per atom.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Diamond is a giant covalent network, so every bond must be broken to melt it.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Iodine is a simple molecular solid held by weak van der Waals forces.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two aluminiums give +6 and three oxygens give −6.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One Mg²⁺ balances two Cl⁻.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ammonium ion forms when nitrogen's lone pair bonds to a proton.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nitrogen donates the whole pair, giving NH₄⁺.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Only N, O and F are electronegative enough to produce hydrogen bonds.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The open hydrogen-bonded lattice makes ice less dense than the liquid.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen bonding is much stronger than the van der Waals forces in similar molecules.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    More electrons give stronger temporary dipoles, which is why iodine is solid and chlorine a gas.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Four bond pairs and no lone pairs give a tetrahedron.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two bond pairs and two lone pairs compress the angle to about 104.5°.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Three bond pairs and one lone pair give a pyramid with an angle of about 107°.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two bond pairs and no lone pairs give a linear molecule.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Three bond pairs repel to 120° in a trigonal planar arrangement.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each silicon is bonded to four oxygens in a continuous network, as in diamond.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    CO₂ exists as separate molecules held together by weak forces.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Displacing a layer puts ions of the same charge side by side, and the lattice splits.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The delocalised electrons continue to bond the displaced layers.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Covalent bonds are intramolecular and far stronger than any intermolecular force.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    H and Cl are both non-metals, so the bond is covalent.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nitrogen forms a triple bond, N≡N.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nitrogen donates its lone pair to the proton.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both have strong bonds throughout a continuous structure.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A metal with a non-metal forms an ionic compound.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Oxygen forms a double bond.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Diamond is a continuous network of carbon atoms.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ionic solid dissociates into mobile ions.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Four bond pairs repel to 109.5°.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Magnesium loses two electrons and each chlorine gains one.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen bonding acts between molecules, not within them.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Methane is a simple molecular gas with very weak intermolecular forces.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Mobile ions appear only on melting.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two bond pairs and no lone pairs give 180°.
  ]

]

